# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1 has "no_return → others_die" and "all_die_eventually → ¬something_alive"; plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "things that partook of life die, remain in the form of death, and do not come to life again", "those other things from which the living spring die too", "all things at last die and are swallowed up in death" and "something is alive" are true, which the second allows; plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3 has "all_die_eventually → ¬something_alive"
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "things that partook of life die, remain in the form of death, and do not come to life again", "those other things from which the living spring die too", "all things at last die and are swallowed up in death" and "something is alive" are true. Is S4, "And in like manner, my dear Cebes, if all things which partook of life were to die, and after they were dead remained in the form of death, and did not come to life again, all would at last die, and nothing would be alive--what other result could there be?", true then?
plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1 has "no_return → living_from_other"; plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.2 has "no_return → living_from_other ∧ others_die" and "all_die_eventually → ¬something_alive"; they differ when only "things that partook of life die, remain in the form of death, and do not come to life again", "the living spring from other things than the dead", "those other things from which the living spring die too", "all things at last die and are swallowed up in death" and "something is alive" are true, which the first allows; plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3 has "no_return → living_from_other ∧ others_die"
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1 has "no_return → ¬something_alive"; plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.2 and plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3 have "no_return → all_die_eventually ∧ ¬something_alive"; they differ when only "things that partook of life die, remain in the form of death, and do not come to life again" is true, which the first allows
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.2 and plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "things that partook of life die, remain in the form of death, and do not come to life again" is true and "all things at last die and are swallowed up in death" and "something is alive" are false. Is the conclusion, "And in like manner, my dear Cebes, if all things which partook of life were to die, and after they were dead remained in the form of death, and did not come to life again, all would at last die, and nothing would be alive--what other result could there be?", true then?
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/negation: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1 P2 (S5): the sentence says "not", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (the living spring from other things than the dead and those other things from which the living spring die too), then all things at last die and are swallowed up in death"
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/negation: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.2 P1 (S5): the sentence says "not", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (the living spring from other things than the dead and those other things from which the living spring die too), then all things at last die and are swallowed up in death"
plato-phaedo-2.md:1: argue/negation: plato-phaedo-2.logic.r1.3 P1 (S5): the sentence says "not", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (the living spring from other things than the dead and those other things from which the living spring die too), then all things at last die and are swallowed up in death"
